<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dominion of India</h1><p class="st-meta">1947 CE – 1950 CE · capital New Delhi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Independent India's first constitutional form, a Commonwealth dominion that integrated the princely states and drafted the constitution of the republic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence-act-1947">Indian Independence Act</a> of 1947, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj</a> was partitioned, and on 15 August 1947 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was created with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> as prime minister. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Lord Mountbatten</a> stayed on as Governor-General until 1948, succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-rajagopalachari">C. Rajagopalachari</a>, the only Indian to hold the post.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dominion confronted partition violence and refugee resettlement, and fought the first war with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Vallabhbhai Patel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/v-p-menon">V. P. Menon</a> integrated more than five hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, using force against Hyderabad in 1948. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> was assassinated in January 1948. A Constituent Assembly chaired in drafting by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> wrote the new constitution.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a> came into force on 26 January 1950, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> a sovereign republic within the Commonwealth and ending the British monarch's role as head of state. The dominion was succeeded by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dominion-of-india.jpg" alt="Dominion of India" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Government of India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_India.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1947 CE – 1950 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>New Delhi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jawaharlal Nehru, Lord Mountbatten, C. Rajagopalachari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hindi, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Union of India (1947-1950), India (dominion)</dd></dl></aside></div>
